@@OLD
whole across an invisible character is read whole. The label is the first
word after any run of characters that are not letters or digits,
ordered-list numbers and task checkboxes, and it is read from each line and
from each paragraph, a line joined with the lines that continue it, since an
inline construct may span lines. The other canonical fields are
@@NEW
whole across an invisible character is read whole; a third reading keeps a
code span's letters and digits and makes its other characters inert, so a
bracket or a tag inside one opens or closes nothing; and the label is also
read after each `<br>` or block-level tag outside code spans, where a
renderer starts a new line, in prose and in the value of a canonical field
line. The label is the first word after any run of characters that are not
letters or digits, ordered-list numbers and task checkboxes, and it is read
from each line and from each run of non-blank lines, joined from every line
of the run, since an inline construct may span lines and a line reader cannot
place every paragraph boundary where a renderer does: a run ends only at a
blank line, a fenced line or an ATX heading, so a boundary the reader misses
or invents can only make a line refuse. The other canonical fields are
@@END
